{
  // Cloudflare Worker with static assets.
  //
  // Cloudflare has retired Pages for new projects, so this app deploys as a
  // Worker: a build emits ./dist, then `npx wrangler deploy` uploads the assets
  // and the Worker in worker/index.js.
  //
  // Deploy with `npm run build:static`, not `npm run build`.
  //
  // build:static also prerenders the public routes to static HTML, which is what
  // lets a crawler's first fetch contain the actual copy instead of an empty
  // <div id="root">. Plain `npm run build` still produces a working site — the
  // Worker falls back to the SPA shell when no snapshot is present — it just
  // ships the SEO handicap that prerendering exists to remove.
  "name": "billtap",
  "main": "worker/index.js",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-29",

  // ── Which BillTap is this, and whose data may it touch ────────────────────
  //
  // There used to be no answer. One BASE44_APP_ID and one master key meant
  // `wrangler dev` on a laptop read and wrote the same Base44 app as
  // billtap.app: trying a change against a receipt created live Session rows
  // next to real restaurants' real bills, and the nightly backup snapshotted
  // them. src/main.jsx made it explicit by hard-coding Sentry's environment to
  // "production", so an exception thrown on a laptop landed in the production
  // error feed looking exactly like a diner's.
  //
  // The top level of this file is production, because that is what a bare
  // `wrangler deploy` uses and a surprise there is the expensive kind. Other
  // environments are declared under "env" below and inherit nothing dangerous:
  // no routes, no crons.
  //
  // PRODUCTION_APP_ID is NOT committed, and both this comment and the one below
  // used to say it was.
  //
  // App ids are not secrets — the frontend bundle has always shipped one in
  // plain text — and recording it is what would let worker/lib/environment.js
  // refuse to serve when a non-production deployment is pointed at the
  // production Base44 app. It appeared in two comments here as a settled fact
  // and in no `vars` block, so that half of the check had never once been able
  // to fire. DATA_BACKEND is unset, which means Base44 is the live database,
  // which means it is the half that currently matters.
  //
  // It is not filled in here because the value is not in this repository to
  // copy. Add it below, alongside PRODUCTION_SUPABASE_URL, and to each env's
  // vars — assertEnvironmentIsolated now refuses to serve a non-production
  // deployment that cannot perform the check, rather than passing quietly, so
  // this is loud until it is done.
  //
  // Master keys are secrets and stay in `wrangler secret put`, per environment.
  //
  // BASE44_APP_ID is set per environment as a secret or a var; there is no
  // default anywhere, because the only sensible default would be production's.
  //
  // PRODUCTION_SUPABASE_URL is committed for the same reason as
  // PRODUCTION_APP_ID: a project ref is not a secret. It is inside every anon
  // key, and the anon key ships in the browser bundle. What it buys is a
  // staging deployment that refuses to serve when it is pointed at production's
  // database — which is the entire reason a staging project exists.
  "vars": {
    "ENVIRONMENT": "production",
    "PRODUCTION_SUPABASE_URL": "https://skrqxxhoxbrvlviqrhjt.supabase.co"
  },

  // ── Environments inherit more than the comment here used to claim ─────────
  //
  // Both blocks below said "no routes, no crons". Neither was true, and wrangler
  // had been saying so on every dry run:
  //
  //   The "env.staging" environment inherits the top-level `routes`
  //   configuration, which includes the custom domain(s): billtap.app.
  //   Deploying this environment will reassign these custom domains away from
  //   the top-level Worker.
  //
  // So `wrangler deploy --env staging` would have taken billtap.app off the
  // production Worker and pointed it at staging. The paragraph above about
  // never answering yes to the route-deletion prompt is about precisely this
  // outcome — the apex domain going away and the printed table-tent QR codes
  // going with it — and the env blocks were a second, quieter way to cause it.
  //
  // `routes` and `triggers` are inheritable in wrangler; `vars` and `unsafe`
  // are not. That asymmetry is why the dangerous things arrived by default and
  // the protective ones did not:
  //
  //   * PRODUCTION_SUPABASE_URL is what assertEnvironmentIsolated compares
  //     against to refuse a non-production deployment pointed at production's
  //     database. Not inherited, so it was absent, so the guard short-circuited
  //     — inert in the only environments it exists for.
  //   * The rate-limit binding is not inherited either, so staging had none.
  //
  // Everything below is now stated per environment rather than assumed. When
  // adding an environment, copy this whole shape; do not trust inheritance to
  // do the safe thing, because it does the opposite.
  "env": {
    // `wrangler deploy --env staging` / `wrangler dev --env staging`.
    "staging": {
      // Empty, NOT absent. Absent means "inherit billtap.app".
      "routes": [],
      "workers_dev": true,
      // Empty for the same reason: crons are inheritable, so staging was
      // registering the 09:00 nightly job. worker/lib/environment.js refuses
      // scheduled work outside production, so nothing ran — but a job that is
      // scheduled and then declines is not the same as one that was never
      // scheduled, and only one of them is what this file said.
      "triggers": { "crons": [] },
      "vars": {
        "ENVIRONMENT": "staging",
        // Repeated deliberately. See assertEnvironmentIsolated: without this
        // the "you are pointed at production" check cannot run at all.
        "PRODUCTION_SUPABASE_URL": "https://skrqxxhoxbrvlviqrhjt.supabase.co"
      },
      "unsafe": {
        "bindings": [
          {
            "name": "API_RATE_LIMITER",
            "type": "ratelimit",
            // A different namespace from production's, or the two share
            // buckets and a load test on staging throttles a real restaurant.
            "namespace_id": "1002",
            "simple": { "limit": 60, "period": 60 }
          }
        ]
      },
      "observability": { "enabled": true }
    },

    // Local work: `wrangler dev --env development`.
    "development": {
      "routes": [],
      "workers_dev": true,
      "triggers": { "crons": [] },
      "vars": {
        "ENVIRONMENT": "development",
        "PRODUCTION_SUPABASE_URL": "https://skrqxxhoxbrvlviqrhjt.supabase.co"
      },
      "unsafe": {
        "bindings": [
          {
            "name": "API_RATE_LIMITER",
            "type": "ratelimit",
            "namespace_id": "1003",
            "simple": { "limit": 60, "period": 60 }
          }
        ]
      },
      "observability": { "enabled": true }
    }
  },

  // Declared here so a deploy stops offering to delete them.
  //
  // These three were created in the dashboard and lived only there. Because
  // this file said nothing about routes, every `wrangler deploy` printed a diff
  // proposing to remove all three — including the apex custom domain — and
  // waited for a yes. Answering yes is the normal thing to do at that prompt,
  // and the one time it did what it threatened, billtap.app stops resolving and
  // the printed table-tent QR codes stop working with it.
  //
  // Mirrors the remote exactly, so deploys are now idempotent rather than a
  // question. Change routing here from now on, not in the dashboard, or the
  // prompt comes back.
  "routes": [
    { "pattern": "billtap.app", "custom_domain": true },
    { "pattern": "billtap.app/*", "zone_name": "billtap.app" },
    { "pattern": "www.billtap.app/*", "zone_name": "billtap.app" }
  ],

  // Gate every deploy on the artefacts actually being complete.
  //
  // wrangler runs build.command before deploy and before dev, and aborts if it
  // exits non-zero — so this catches `npx wrangler deploy` typed straight into a
  // terminal, not just the npm script.
  //
  // It exists because the opposite happened: build:static is
  // `vite build && node scripts/prerender.mjs`, the prerender step died on a
  // missing Playwright browser, and vite had already written a valid dist/
  // without snapshots. A follow-up `wrangler deploy` shipped it, and the only
  // symptom in production was the wrong <title>.
  // Rate limiting on the unauthenticated /api endpoints.
  //
  // LIVE. It was committed commented out so that a deploy could not fail on a
  // binding an account might not have; it is on now because it was wanted on,
  // and because the keying it depends on has since been fixed.
  //
  // That fix matters more than the limit itself. Every endpoint used to be
  // keyed on CF-Connecting-IP, and six people at a table are ONE address — a
  // restaurant's wifi is one NAT, and on a busy night it is every table in the
  // room. Claiming items, tapping paid and polling for who else has settled
  // runs to hundreds of calls a minute from that single address, all of it
  // legitimate. Switched on as it was, the first thing this would have done in
  // production is 429 a paying customer's diners mid-meal.
  //
  // worker/lib/rate-limit.js now keys the table endpoints — joinSession,
  // markMePaid, verifyQRToken, getSplitStatus and scan-receipt — per
  // participant, and leaves the endpoints that spend money on the address:
  // rating-alert sends an SMS against an account with no spend cap,
  // restaurant-lead sends email, createSession writes rows.
  //
  // 60 per minute per key. It still no-ops if the binding is absent, so
  // commenting this back out is a safe way to switch it off in a hurry.
  //
  // If a deploy fails here with an unknown binding type, this account does not
  // have the ratelimit binding: comment the block out again and nothing else
  // changes.
  "unsafe": {
    "bindings": [
      {
        "name": "API_RATE_LIMITER",
        "type": "ratelimit",
        "namespace_id": "1001",
        "simple": { "limit": 60, "period": 60 }
      }
    ]
  },

  // Logs and errors, searchable in the Cloudflare dashboard.
  //
  // Without this the only way to see a failure was to be running `wrangler
  // tail` at the exact moment it happened. That is how the app spent an unknown
  // stretch of time unable to perform a single core operation — every
  // functions.invoke answering "Functions are blocked" — while the prerendered
  // marketing pages carried on rendering perfectly and nothing reported a
  // problem.
  //
  // The console.error calls in worker/routes/functions.js name the function
  // that failed, so this makes "fn/createSession failed: ..." searchable after
  // the fact rather than only in the moment.
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },

  // Two schedules, and the separation is not cosmetic.
  //
  // 09:00 UTC — after close in America/Los_Angeles, so a day's sessions are
  // complete when the snapshot is taken.
  //
  // ── Why the retention pass gets its own ─────────────────────────────────
  //
  // Both jobs used to run from the single 09:00 invocation, one after the other
  // through waitUntil. That put them in one Worker invocation, which means one
  // subrequest budget between them — and both are the kind of job that spends
  // it in bulk. The backup pages every entity (up to 200 pages each, eight
  // entities), and the retention pass does a write and a storage delete per
  // session plus the orphan sweep's deletes. Whichever ran second would be the
  // one that hit the ceiling, silently, in the middle of a night nobody was
  // watching — and "silently" is the whole complaint about the backup this
  // replaced.
  //
  // Separate schedules mean separate invocations and separate budgets. The
  // handler in worker/index.js dispatches on event.cron.
  //
  // 09:00 backup first, 09:30 retention second, deliberately in that order: a
  // restore taken from the night's snapshot holds the data as it was BEFORE the
  // names were removed, not after.
  "triggers": {
    "crons": ["0 9 * * *", "30 9 * * *"]
  },

  // The bucket the backup writes to.
  //
  // COMMENTED OUT because no bucket exists yet. Create one and uncomment:
  //
  //     npx wrangler r2 bucket create billtap-backups
  //
  // Until then the cron fires, throws "BACKUP_BUCKET is not bound", and the
  // invocation is marked failed — deliberately. The failure this replaces was a
  // backup that reported success and stored nothing, so an unconfigured backup
  // has to be loud rather than quiet. src/RUNBOOK.md states plainly that there
  // is no restorable backup; keep that warning until this binding is live.
  //
  // "r2_buckets": [
  //   { "binding": "BACKUP_BUCKET", "bucket_name": "billtap-backups" }
  // ],

  "build": {
    "command": "node scripts/verify-dist.mjs"
  },

  "assets": {
    "directory": "./dist",
    "binding": "ASSETS",

    // Deep links must boot the SPA rather than 404 — billtap.app/restaurants
    // from the flyer QR, and /r/<slug> from a table tent.
    "not_found_handling": "single-page-application",

    // Start from a catch-all and only ever SUBTRACT. Never replace this with a
    // positive allow-list such as ["/api/*"].
    //
    // Two things depend on the catch-all. Obviously: /api/* would otherwise be
    // answered by the SPA fallback with index.html, and every API call —
    // including the Base44 data layer the whole app runs on — would silently
    // return HTML instead of JSON.
    //
    // Less obviously: "not_found_handling": "single-page-application" makes the
    // assets binding match *every* path, so an allow-list means the Worker is
    // never invoked for page requests at all. SPA_ROUTES, LEGACY_REDIRECTS, the
    // prerendered snapshots, the real 404 statuses and the security headers all
    // become unreachable in production while the unit tests still pass, because
    // those call the Worker directly and never exercise this dispatch. That
    // exact bug shipped once. worker/index.test.mjs now asserts the shape.
    //
    // The negations are a cost control: with a bare `true` every hashed bundle,
    // icon and image is a billable Worker invocation, and none of them need
    // anything the Worker does — they cannot be framed, and nothing redirects.
    // Verified under `wrangler dev`: /restaurants and /junk go through the
    // Worker (security headers present, 404 rewritten), /assets/* does not.
    //
    // Only ever exclude static directories. Excluding a page route would
    // silently disable its redirect, 404 and headers — the test checks for that
    // too. The tradeoff of excluding them: a missing file under these paths now
    // returns the SPA shell with a 200 rather than a 404. Crawlers do not index
    // hashed bundles, so that is an acceptable trade.
    "run_worker_first": ["/*", "!/assets/*", "!/icons/*", "!/img/*", "!/video/*"]
  }
}
